PPMd var.H, by Dmitry Shkarin.

The C implementation that used to sit in this directory -- Model.cpp,
SubAlloc.hpp, Coder.hpp, PPMd.h, PPMdType.h -- has been replaced by the Rust
port in rust/darc-codecs/src/ppmd/, and C_PPMD.cpp is now only the
COMPRESSION_METHOD wrapper around it. The port is byte-identical to the C as
DArc built it, checked per push by rust/difftest/ppmd-check.sh and
ppmd-alloc-check.sh against a pinned revision of the deleted sources.

FreeArc's changes to Shkarin's original, which the port reproduces:

1. In PPMdType.h the PRIME_STREAM class is used, configured to use callbacks
2. BOOL _STDCALL StartSubAllocator(UINT t) - now receives the memory size in bytes, not megabytes
3. static void _STDCALL StartModelRare(int _MaxOrder,MR_METHOD _MRMethod)
   in order to replace ::MaxOrder/::MRMethod with MaxOrder/MRMethod
4. Maximum model order: MAX_O=128
5. Compiles only with -O1 !

Point 5 was understating it, and it is the most important thing on this list.
StateCpy and SWAP type-pun through `(WORD&)` references, which violates strict
aliasing, and rescale()'s `if (p->Freq == 0)` then reads either the value just
assigned in the loop or the slot the bubble sort has since overwritten --
depending on what the compiler is allowed to assume. The two readings produce
DIFFERENT COMPRESSED BYTES. Measured:

    -O1                        reuses      -O0                        re-reads
    -O2                        reuses      -O1 -fno-strict-aliasing   re-reads

The makefile passed -fno-strict-aliasing as well as -O1, so every archive DArc
has written came from the re-reading build, and that is what the port does.
The Rust has no aliasing question to answer: a load is a load.
